              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "When receiving or accepting food deliveries, the safe temperature depends on the type of food. Under the **FDA Food Code** and **ServSafe** standards, food temperatures are strictly monitored to keep them out of the \"Temperature Danger Zone\" (41\u00b0F to 135\u00b0F) where bacteria grow most rapidly.\n\nThe standard temperatures at which food can be safely accepted are outlined below:\n\n### Quick Reference: Food Receiving Temperatures\n\n| Food Category | Safe Receiving Temperature | Key Action Upon Receipt |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cold TCS Foods** (Meat, poultry, dairy, cut produce) | **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower** | Put in cold storage immediately. |\n| **Hot TCS Foods** (Cooked/prepared foods) | **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher** | Keep hot or cool down safely. |\n| **Frozen Foods** | **Frozen solid** | Put in freezer immediately; reject if thawing. |\n| **Milk** | **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower** | Cool to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours. |\n| **Shell Eggs** | **Air temperature of 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower** | Store immediately at 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower. |\n| **Shucked Shellfish** | **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower** | Cool to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours. |\n| **Live Shellfish** (Oysters, clams, mussels) | **Air temp of 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) / Internal <= 50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)** | Cool to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours. |\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Guidelines by Category\n\n#### 1. Cold TCS Foods (41\u00b0F / 5\u00b0C or lower)\n\"TCS\" stands for **Time/Temperature Control for Safety**. This includes fresh meats, poultry, fish, seafood, dairy, pre-cut melons, cut tomatoes, and leafy greens. \n* **Note for Home Consumers:** If you are a home consumer accepting grocery deliveries, the USDA recommends a slightly lower threshold of **40\u00b0F (4\u00b0C) or lower** for refrigerated foods.\n\n#### 2. Hot TCS Foods (135\u00b0F / 57\u00b0C or higher)\nIf you are receiving pre-cooked hot foods (like catering deliveries), they must arrive at **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**. *(USDA home guidelines specify 140\u00b0F or higher).*\n\n#### 3. Frozen Foods (Frozen Solid)\nThere is no specific numeric temperature for frozen food, but it **must be received frozen solid**. \n* **When to reject:** Reject frozen deliveries if you see signs of thawing and refreezing, such as fluid/water stains on the bottom of the packaging or large ice crystals on the food/packaging (which indicate the food warmed up and refroze).\n\n#### 4. The \"45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)\" Exceptions\nA few specific items can legally be accepted at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)**, provided they are cooled down to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours** of delivery:\n* **Milk**\n* **Shell Eggs:** Must be received at an ambient (air) temperature of 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower. \n* **Shucked Shellfish:** Must be 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower.\n* **Live Shellfish (Oysters, Clams, Mussels, Scallops):** Must be received at an air temperature of 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) and an internal temperature no higher than 50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C). Once received, they must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours.\n\n---\n\n### How to Check the Temperature Upon Delivery\n1. **Meat, Poultry, and Fish:** Insert a clean, calibrated thermometer probe directly into the thickest part of the food (usually the center).\n2. **Reduced Oxygen Packaging (ROP) / Sous Vide:** Wrap the packaging around the thermometer probe, or place the probe between two packages. *Do not puncture the packaging*.\n3. **Other Packaged Foods (like milk cartons):** Open a carton and insert the thermometer probe into the liquid, or place the probe between two packages in the case.",
